---
description: StateRoot persistent project state protocol — resume, checkpoint, handoff
globs:
alwaysApply: true
---

This project uses StateRoot for persistent, harness-neutral project state (`.stateroot/`).

1. At session start, prefer the auto-injected StateRoot digest from hooks when present. Only run `stateroot resume --harness cursor` if no digest appeared yet. Do not run resume twice unless the user asks (`--force` reprints).
2. After every step that changes project state — files written, decisions made, milestones, blockers — run `stateroot checkpoint --note "<what changed and why>"`. Hooks do not fire on every write.
3. Before attempting a non-trivial approach, check the failed-approaches log with `stateroot memory recall "failed approach <topic>"` and do not repeat recorded failures.
4. After meaningful real-tree changes, run `stateroot snap`; use `stateroot revert` / `stateroot fork` for verified restoration or divergent work.
4. Before ending a session, when approaching usage limits, or when asked to switch harness, prefer `stateroot handoff write --from cursor [--to <harness>] --objective "…" --task "…" --context-summary "…" [--next "…"]`, **or** rely on hook finalize when it ran. Omit `--to` for continuity-only. Use `--input` only for large payloads. Never write under `.stateroot/handoffs/` by hand. Thin fields warn; they do not refuse the write.
5. Learnings are taste, not facts. Write a judgment (`prefer X over Y` / `never Z`) plus when it applies — not a stack inventory. Scopes: `--user`, `--workspace`, project, `--domain <slug>`. Facts go to `memory_save`. Everything activates immediately — no classify→approve story.
6. Shared rules: product-intent is always on (full body in the digest). `stateroot rules sync` imports other harness rules into the same pool. Do not add classifiers, approval gates, or generic architecture.
7. Never edit files under `.stateroot/` directly; all state access goes through the `stateroot` CLI. The CLI is offline-safe and queues operations locally when the network is down.
8. Privacy: root `.gitignore` and `.staterootignore` (plus hardcoded `.git/` and `.stateroot/local/`) gate snap/root trees. `.staterootignore` is extra patterns for things git still tracks.
